    "# Why We Need Statistics in Data Science\n",
    "\n",
    "*A hands-on walk-through, built from Dr. Abhinanda Sarkar's classroom session (Great Learning).*\n",
    "\n",
    "Data science and machine learning have been called the hardest job of the 21st\n",
    "century. Whatever the title, one thing is constant underneath all of it:\n",
    "**statistics is the language you use to reason about data you cannot fully see.**\n",
    "\n",
    "With statistics we make claims like *\"sales will fall most in this customer\n",
    "segment next quarter\"* or *\"there's a 16% chance this positive HIV test is a true\n",
    "positive.\"* That isn't magic and it isn't a crystal ball - it's just statistics.\n",
    "\n",
    "This notebook is a guided tour. Every idea is paired with **runnable code** so you\n",
    "can change a number and watch the idea respond. The arc is:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Two ways of thinking** - statistics vs. machine learning\n",
    "2. **Descriptive → Predictive → Prescriptive** - the three jobs of analytics\n",
    "3. **Descriptive statistics** - mean vs. median, spread, skew, outliers\n",
    "4. **Correlation & covariance** - how two variables move together\n",
    "5. **Linear regression as description** - summarizing many variables at once\n",
    "6. **Probability** - coping with uncertainty\n",
    "7. **Conditional probability & Bayes' theorem** - spam filters and medical tests\n",
    "8. **Distributions** - binomial, Poisson, and the normal distribution\n",
    "\n",
    "Run the next cell first - it sets up everything."
   ]
