    "Same average, very different consistency - and standard deviation is what reveals it.\n",
    "\n",
    "\n",
    "### When to use it\n",
    "- Measuring **consistency or risk** - wait times, scores, prices, returns.\n",
    "- Comparing groups with **similar averages** to see which is more stable.\n",
    "- Symmetric/bell-shaped data, where the **68–95–99.7 rule** holds (68% within 1 SD of the mean, 95% within 2, 99.7% within 3).\n",
    "\n",
    "**Avoid it when** data is **skewed or has big outliers** (e.g. incomes) — squaring lets one extreme value dominate. Use **median + IQR** instead.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why data scientists use it\n",
    "\n",
    "**1. Spotting outliers & fraud**\n",
    "- Meaning: a value far from the average (more than ~3 SD away) is suspicious.\n",
    "- Example: a card normally spends `$50`; a sudden `$9,000` charge is way outside the spread → flagged as fraud.\n",
    "- Takeaway: SD draws the line between \"normal\" and \"weird.\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**2. Putting features on one scale (Z-score)**\n",
    "- Meaning: rescale every column with `z = (value − mean) / SD` so they sit on the same ruler.\n",
    "- Example: \"Age\" (0–100) and \"Income\" ($0–$100,000) - without scaling, the model over-weights income.\n",
    "- Takeaway: SD lets the model treat every variable fairly.\n",
    "\n",
    "**3. Checking if a model is reliable**\n",
    "- Meaning: look at the spread (SD) of the model's mistakes, not just the average error.\n",
    "- Example: a weather app off by \"5 min ± 1\" is trustworthy; off by \"5 min ± 40\" is useless.\n",
    "- Takeaway: low SD of errors = consistently accurate.\n",
    "\n",
    "**4. A/B testing - real win or luck?**\n",
    "- Meaning: SD sets the \"margin of error\" around each result.\n",
    "- Example: a new web page gets 12% clicks vs the old 10% - if that gap beats the SD, it's a real improvement.\n",
    "- Takeaway: SD tells you when a difference is trustworthy, not random.\n",
    "\n",
    "**5. Quick feel for new data (EDA)**\n",
    "- Meaning: a glance at each column's SD shows which ones vary a lot.\n",
    "- Example: \"Age\" SD = 7 (tight) vs \"Income\" SD = 14,000 (very spread) - income carries more signal.\n",
    "- Takeaway: high SD flags variables worth a closer look.\n",
