    "### Left chart - the histogram (the shape of all ages)\n",
    "\n",
    "Think of it as sorting every customer into age \"buckets\" and stacking them up:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Each **bar** is an age range. The **taller** the bar, the **more people** are that age.\n",
    "- You can see most bars are bunched on the **left** (the 20s). The tallest bar is around age 26 - that's the most common age.\n",
    "- As you move right (older ages), the bars get **short** - only a handful of customers are in their 40s and 50s.\n",
    "\n",
    "So the story: **most customers are young (early-to-mid 20s), with a long thin tail of older folks stretching to the right.** That right-hand tail is what \"right-skewed\" means.\n",
    "\n",
    "The two **dashed lines** show the two kinds of \"average\":\n",
    "- Green (median, 27) = the *middle* customer's age.\n",
    "- Red (mean, 28.5) = the *arithmetic average* age.\n",
    "\n",
    "The red line sits a little **to the right** of the green line. That gap is the proof of the skew - those few older customers drag the average up, even though the typical person is 27. (This is exactly why median is often the more honest \"typical\" value.)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Right chart - the box plots (comparing men vs. women)\n",
    "\n",
    "Same age data, but now **split into two groups** so we can compare. Each box summarizes one group:\n",
    "\n",
    "- The **box** holds the **middle 50%** of that group (from the 25% mark to the 75% mark).\n",
    "- The **green line inside** the box is that group's **median** (typical age).\n",
    "- The **whiskers** (the vertical lines sticking out) reach the normal low and high ages.\n",
    "- The **little circles at the top** are **outliers** - unusually old customers who stand apart from the rest.\n",
    "\n",
    "What it tells us: **men and women have almost the same age profile** - both medians sit around 26–27, both boxes cover roughly 23–32. The main difference is the **male group has more older outliers** (those circles climbing toward 50).\n",
    "\n",
    "### The big picture - why bother?\n",
    "\n",
    "We're doing a quick \"**who are our customers?**\" health check before any serious analysis. In two glances we now know:\n",
    "\n",
    "1. Our typical buyer is in their **mid-to-late 20s**.\n",
    "2. The data **leans young** with a few older buyers (right skew).\n",
    "3. **Gender barely changes the age picture** - so we probably don't need separate age strategies for men and women.\n",
    "\n",
    "That's the whole point: a couple of pictures reveal the shape, the center, and the oddballs in the data **faster and more clearly than any table of numbers could.**"
