    "## 7. Percentile , Quartiles, IQR (Interquartile Range)\n",
    "\n",
    "### 1. Percentile - \"what % is below me?\"\n",
    "\n",
    "A percentile answers: *out of everyone, what fraction scored lower than this point?*\n",
    "\n",
    "In your data, **Income** ranges from about `$30,000` to `$105,000`. If we say *\"the 75th percentile of income is `$62,575` ,\"* it means **75% of your customers earn less than $62,575**, and only the top 25% earn more.\n",
    "\n",
    "Real-life feel: it's the same idea as a school result - *\"you're in the 90th percentile\"* means you beat 90% of students. Or a baby's growth chart - *\"50th percentile for height\"* means exactly average height for that age.\n",
    "\n",
    "### 2. Quartiles - slicing the data into 4 equal groups\n",
    "\n",
    "Quartiles are just three special percentiles that cut your sorted data into **four equal quarters**:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Q1 = 25th percentile** -> for Age, that's **~24**. A quarter of customers are younger than 24.\n",
    "- **Q2 = 50th percentile = median** -> Age **27**. The exact middle customer.\n",
    "- **Q3 = 75th percentile** -> Age **32**. Three-quarters are younger than 32.\n",
    "\n",
    "So your customers split like this by age:\n",
    "```\n",
    "youngest 25%  |  next 25%  |  next 25%  |  oldest 25%\n",
    "   19–24      |   24–27    |   27–32    |    32–50\n",
    "            (Q1)       (median)      (Q3)\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "### 3. IQR - the spread of the \"middle crowd\"\n",
    "\n",
    "**IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 32 − 24 = 8 years.**\n",
    "\n",
    "It's the age range covered by the **middle 50%** of customers - ignoring the youngest quarter and the oldest quarter. It tells you how tightly the *typical* group is packed, **without letting the extremes interfere.**\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why use them - grounded in your data\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reason 1: they don't get fooled by outliers.**\n",
    "Look at your Income column - most people earn `$40k`–`$60k`, but a few earn `$84,300`, `$91,000`, even `$105,000`. Those few high earners drag the **mean** income up. The **median** (`$53,350`) and the quartiles barely move, because they only care about *who's in the middle of the line*, not how rich the richest person is. So for income, the median + IQR tell the honest \"typical customer\" story.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reason 2: they tell you \"where do I stand?\"**\n",
    "Customer #1 earns `$52,000`. On its own that number means little. But knowing it's near the **median** instantly tells you he's a perfectly average earner — not rich, not poor. That ranking is the useful part.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reason 3: IQR is a clean, outlier-proof measure of spread.**\n",
    "Standard deviation squares distances, so one `$105,000` earner inflates it. IQR uses only the middle 50%, so those extremes can't distort it.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Reason 4: they power automatic outlier detection.**\n",
    "Using the **fence rule** on Age: lower fence = Q1 − 1.5×IQR = 24 − 12 = 12; upper fence = Q3 + 1.5×IQR = 32 + 12 = 44. Any customer older than 44 gets flagged - and indeed your 45-to-50-year-olds (like customer #0 at 42 is fine, but a 50-year-old) are the outliers. This is exactly how the dots appear on a box plot.\n",
    "\n",
    "### When to check / where to use (in this dataset)\n",
    "\n",
    "| In your data | Use this | Why |\n",
    "|---|---|---|\n",
    "| **Income, Miles** (skewed - long tail of high values) | **Median + IQR** | A few big earners / high-mileage users would distort mean + std |\n",
    "| **Fitness, Education** (symmetric, ratings 1–5) | **Mean + std dev** | Balanced, so the mean is honest |\n",
    "| Ranking a customer (\"top 10% earners to target premium model\") | **Percentile** | Directly gives position |\n",
    "| Cleaning data before modeling (\"flag weird ages/incomes\") | **IQR fences** | Robust, standard anomaly rule |\n",
    "| Drawing a **box plot** of Age by Gender | **Quartiles + IQR** | The box = Q1–Q3, whiskers = the fences |\n",
    "\n",
    "### Your one rule of thumb\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Symmetric, clean data -> mean + standard deviation.**\n",
    "> **Skewed data or outliers -> median + IQR + percentiles.**\n",
    "\n",
    "In your treadmill data, **Age and especially Income lean right** (a few older, higher-earning customers), so the **median and IQR describe your typical customer more truthfully** than mean ± std. That's why both toolsets exist - you pick the one that won't lie about *your* particular column.\n",
    "\n",
    "Want me to write the code that builds a full quartile + IQR + outlier summary for **every numeric column** in this dataset (Age, Income, Miles, etc.), so you can see it all at once?"
