    "## 8. Covariance and Correlation \n",
    "\n",
    "## The core idea: do two things move together?\n",
    "\n",
    "Everything in this section answers one question:\n",
    "\n",
    "> **When one thing goes up, does the other go up too, go down, or do nothing?**\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, in your data: *when a customer's Usage goes up, do their Miles go up too?* (Yes — people who use the treadmill more walk more miles.) That \"move together\" relationship is what covariance and correlation measure.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Covariance - the raw \"do they move together?\" number\n",
    "\n",
    "**Covariance** checks, for each customer, whether two values are *both above average* or *both below average* at the same time.\n",
    "\n",
    "- If high-Usage customers also tend to be high-Miles customers -> **positive covariance** (they rise together).\n",
    "- If high-Income customers tend to be low-Usage -> **negative covariance** (one up, other down).\n",
    "- If there's no pattern -> **covariance near zero**.\n",
    "\n",
    "**The problem with covariance:** its size depends on the units, so the raw number is almost meaningless. Covariance between Income (in dollars) and Miles will be a huge number just because income is in the tens of thousands — not because the relationship is strong. You can tell the **direction** (+ or −) but not the **strength**. That's why we rarely use covariance directly.\n",
    "\n",
    "### Correlation — covariance cleaned up\n",
    "\n",
    "**Correlation** is just covariance with the units stripped out, so it always lands between **−1 and +1**. Now the number is meaningful and comparable across any pair of columns:\n",
    "\n",
    "```\n",
    "−1 ........... 0 ........... +1\n",
    "strong         no          strong\n",
    "opposite     relationship   together\n",
    "```\n",
    "\n",
    "- **+1** = perfect positive - when one goes up, the other *always* goes up.\n",
    "- **+0.7** = strong positive - usually go up together.\n",
    "- **0** = no linear relationship.\n",
    "- **−0.7** = strong negative - one up, other down.\n",
    "- **−1** = perfect negative.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Real-life examples:**\n",
    "- Height and weight -> **+0.7** (taller people tend to weigh more).\n",
    "- Hours studied and exam score -> **positive**.\n",
    "- Car weight and fuel mileage -> **negative** (heavier cars get fewer miles per gallon).\n",
    "- Shoe size and exam score -> **~0** (no relationship).\n",
    "\n",
    "In treadmill data: **how often someone uses it (Usage)** and **how many miles they walk (Miles)** go up together - strong positive correlation. Makes sense, right?\n",
    "\n",
    "### Where it's used (real-world)\n",
    "\n",
    "| Field | What correlation does |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Finance** | Build portfolios - combine assets that *don't* move together (low correlation) to reduce risk |\n",
    "| **Machine learning** | Find which features relate to the target before modelling; drop redundant features that are highly correlated with each other |\n",
    "| **Marketing** | See what relates to spending (income? age? visits?) to target the right customers |\n",
    "| **Healthcare** | Check if a risk factor moves with a disease (e.g. blood pressure vs. age) |\n",
    "| **Product (your data)** | Discover that Miles is driven by Usage and Fitness -> design and market the treadmill around those |\n",
    "\n",
    "### When to use it\n",
    "\n",
    "- When you have **two numeric columns** and want to know if they're related.\n",
    "- During **exploratory analysis (EDA)** - a correlation matrix / heatmap gives you a one-glance map of *every* pairwise relationship in the dataset.\n",
    "- Before **modelling** - to pick useful predictors and spot redundant ones.\n",
    "\n",
    "(Note: correlation only works on **numbers**, and only catches **straight-line** relationships. A curved relationship like price vs. profit going up then down - can have correlation near 0 even though the variables are strongly related.)\n",
    "\n",
    "### Why to use it\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **It compresses a relationship into one number** you can compare across all pairs of columns.\n",
    "2. **It guides modelling** - tells you which inputs matter for predicting your target (Miles).\n",
    "3. **It reveals structure** - a heatmap of correlations shows the whole dataset's relationships at a glance.\n",
    "\n",
    "### The one critical warning: correlation ≠ causation\n",
    "\n",
    "**Two things moving together does NOT mean one causes the other.**\n",
    "\n",
    "Famous example: in summer, **ice cream sales go up AND drowning deaths go up** - together. So does ice cream cause drowning? No! The real reason is **hot weather** — it makes people buy ice cream *and* go swimming. The two just happen to rise together.\n",
    "\n",
    "So correlation tells you *\"these move together\"* - it never proves *\"this one causes that one.\"* Always ask: *is there a hidden reason behind both?*\n",
    "\n",
    "> **Correlation is a score from −1 to +1 that tells you if two things go up together (+), go opposite (−), or aren't connected (0). It's super useful for spotting patterns - but it never proves one thing causes the other.**"
   ]
